# In gitattributes the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all goes FIRST and the
# exceptions go after it. Written the other way round this file silently pinned run.cmd
# to LF and a fresh clone reproduced BUG-010 on the first command (BUG-013).
#
# Default: LF. Fixture and label text is byte-compared against stored raw_text, so a
# line-ending rewrite would invalidate every citation offset (BUG-006).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Exception, and the reason this file exists. Batch files are read by cmd.exe using byte
# offsets: with LF-only endings it seeks into the middle of a token and fails with errors
# like "'tlocal' is not recognized". Pinned so a clone on any platform, or any
# core.autocrlf setting, cannot break the launcher.
#
# Verify after any edit here — the attribute, not the file on disk, is what a clone gets:
#   git check-attr text eol -- run.cmd     ->  text: auto   eol: crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.db binary
